@page "/docs/search"
@attribute [Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization.Authorize]
@using ALDevToolbox.Components.Shared

@*
    What the "Search or jump to" box does, for someone who has never used one.
    Linked from the box's own footer and reachable from it as a page, which is
    why it is in NavDestinations. See .design/command-palette.md.

    Page archetype 12 (docs / long-form): DocsPage over a .prose column with an
    on-page table of contents.

    Tone: the reader has just met a search box that answers with a list of
    customers and pages, and wants to know whether it can break anything. That
    question is answered on the page, not implied. Nothing here names a class, a
    file or an endpoint - the reader is a consultant, not a maintainer.
*@

<PageTitle>Search or jump to · AL Workbench</PageTitle>

<DocsPage Title="Search or jump to"
          Subtitle="One box that finds a customer, an environment, a release or a page - and takes you there."
          Toc="@Toc">
    <p class="prose__lead">
        The search box in the top bar is a shortcut for getting somewhere. Type
        part of what you are looking for, press Enter, and you are on the page.
        It is quicker than the menu once you know the name of the thing you
        want, and it is the fastest way to a customer you only half remember.
    </p>

    <h2 id="search-open">Opening it</h2>
    <p>
        Click <strong>Search or jump to...</strong> in the top bar. On a
        keyboard you can also press <kbd class="kbd">Ctrl</kbd> +
        <kbd class="kbd">K</kbd> from anywhere in AL Workbench
        (<kbd class="kbd">Cmd</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">K</kbd> on a Mac), which
        works even while you are typing in an editor. On a phone or a tablet the
        button is the way in.
    </p>
    <p>
        With nothing typed, the box lists every page you can open, so you can
        browse it like a menu. Start typing and the list narrows.
    </p>

    <h2 id="search-finds">What it finds</h2>
    <p>
        Results are grouped, and the group tells you what kind of thing you are
        looking at:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Solutions</strong> - by the customer's name or the short
            name your team uses for them. You can also find a Solution by the
            name or company of a contact on it, by its Voice account number, or
            by its Business Central tenant ID - useful mid-call, when the
            customer gives you a number rather than a name. The row shows you
            which of those matched, never the number itself.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Environments</strong> - by environment name, with the customer underneath.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Releases</strong> - the releases in Object Explorer, by
            label, Business Central version, country, or the customer whose
            build produced them.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Recipes</strong> - the AL recipes in the Cookbook, by title or tag.</li>
        <li><strong>Go to</strong> - every page in the menu.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Every word you type has to match something, but the order does not
        matter: <code>con prod</code> and <code>prod con</code> both find the
        production environment of CRONUS Consulting. Capitals and accents are
        ignored, so <code>moller</code> finds M&oslash;ller. Spelling, though, is
        not guessed at - if nothing comes back, check it.
    </p>
    <div class="note note--info">
        <span class="note__icon"><Icon Name="info" Width="17" Height="17" /></span>
        <span class="note__title">You only ever see your own work</span>
        <span class="note__body">
            The box searches exactly what you could reach by clicking. A private
            Solution you are not a member of does not appear here at all - if you
            expect a customer and get nothing, the Solutions page will tell you
            whether it exists and who to ask.
        </span>
    </div>
    <p>
        It does not search inside code, translations or the text of a recipe.
        Object Explorer and the Cookbook have their own search for that, and
        they find more than this box does.
    </p>

    <h2 id="search-keys">Choosing a result</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><kbd class="kbd">&uarr;</kbd> and <kbd class="kbd">&darr;</kbd> move between results. The mouse does the same thing.</li>
        <li><kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd> opens the highlighted one.</li>
        <li><kbd class="kbd">Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd> opens it in a new tab.</li>
        <li><kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd> closes the box and leaves you where you were.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="prose__cap">
        Middle-click or Ctrl-click opens a result in a new tab.
    </p>

    <h2 id="search-safe">It only takes you places</h2>
    <p>
        Every result is somewhere to go. Nothing you type here publishes,
        deletes or updates a customer's environment - those actions stay on the
        pages that own them, behind the confirmation that names the environment.
    </p>
    <div class="note note--tip">
        <span class="note__icon"><Icon Name="lightbulb" Width="17" Height="17" /></span>
        <span class="note__title">You can search freely</span>
        <span class="note__body">
            Typing a customer's name and opening the wrong result costs nothing -
            the back button puts you straight back.
            Nothing in this box changes anything.
        </span>
    </div>

    <h2 id="search-nothing">When it comes back empty</h2>
    <p>
        Two different things can happen, and they say different things:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>No search results</strong> - nothing matched what you typed.
            Try fewer words, or check the spelling.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Search is not available right now</strong> - the search did
            not come back, usually a brief network hiccup. The menu still works,
            so you can get where you are going; try again in a moment.
        </li>
    </ul>
</DocsPage>

@code {
    private static readonly DocsTocEntry[] Toc =
    {
        new("Opening it", "search-open"),
        new("What it finds", "search-finds"),
        new("Choosing a result", "search-keys"),
        new("It only takes you places", "search-safe"),
        new("When it comes back empty", "search-nothing"),
    };
}
